1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,160
plus B times the dummy variable, plus, say,

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:06,240
gamma times the vector of controls.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,440
And rational estimation says that B will be equal to 0.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,560
There will be no difference in the average opinion

5
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:13,160
of experts in the public, right?

6
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,200
Because in the rational estimation's world,

7
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,480
what is the effect of knowledge?

8
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,680
Is it to shift means?

9
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:21,920
No, it's to compress variance.

10
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,120
So if that's the truth, capital T star,

11
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:28,000
then ignorant people have that distribution,

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,800
and informed people have that distribution.

13
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,760
That's what rational estimations is all about.

14
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:36,280
Or again, so why the controls?

15
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:38,040
The controls are there just because there's

16
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,160
a lot of people who think that economics

17
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:43,080
is a fake discipline, or at least a biased discipline.

18
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:47,760
And so the controls are there to deal with the objections.

19
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,680
And there are two main reasons people

20
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:52,480
look for for mistrusting economists.

21
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:56,160
So one is that economists have some kind

22
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:57,160
of self-serving bias.

23
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:59,000
So maybe it just means that if they're rich

24
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,520
they got on job security, so they're

25
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:02,320
out of touch with the American people,

26
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,560
out of touch with the problems of the American people.

27
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:06,920
So of course they think that international trade

28
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:08,880
or downsizing are good when they personally

29
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:12,240
are immune to the bad part, as well, the bad stuff.

30
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,920
So that is a story, but it's a testable story.

31
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,600
Because you can get measures of whatever

32
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,360
it is that supposedly is biasing economists' judgment,

33
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,720
and then we put that control variable in.

34
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:25,320
And when you do that, that is like going and pairing

35
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:28,520
an economist with high income and job security

36
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:32,120
to a non-economist with high income and job security.

37
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,040
Or alternately, it's like comparing a normal person

38
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:37,600
with someone with economic training,

39
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:40,160
but all the problems of a normal person.

40
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:42,560
That's the whole idea of putting in the control variables.

41
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:44,080
And again, if this theory is right,

42
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,480
it is driven by personal circumstances,

43
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:49,160
then that should make the gap go away.

44
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:52,720
But in practice, it almost never does.

45
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,260
And then another story that's been told of ideological bias,

46
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:56,800
this one just says that economics

47
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:00,240
is a coven of extreme right-wing ideology.

48
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,880
And people believe it because if you don't believe it,

49
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,320
you don't go into the discipline.

50
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,200
So of course, of course economists

51
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:08,240
believe this horrible stuff.

52
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,640
And again, this is something where you go and get measures

53
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:13,720
of people's ideology or party, and then

54
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:15,880
try to see whether that explains the difference.

55
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:18,920
So if you are a very liberal democratic economist,

56
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:20,520
do you then believe the same stuff

57
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,880
that very liberal democratic non-economists believe?

58
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:26,440
And again, the answer is totally not.

59
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:28,640
Paul Krugman's beliefs are very different from that

60
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:32,200
of the people that he claims to speak for.

61
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:34,680
And one of his main goals, especially in earlier years,

62
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,000
was to try to fix other people on his side

63
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,880
and introduce them to economics.

64
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,840
So that is an ideal.

65
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:46,440
People often have the idea, well, look,

66
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:47,640
all this free market stuff, that's

67
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:48,760
just the right-wing people.

68
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:50,680
It's like, well, no, actually, a lot of this

69
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,440
is stuff that almost all economists believe.

70
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,480
Right now, a safer this is just to say, well,

71
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:57,720
economists are right-wing regardless of what they say.

72
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:04,400
So Paul Krugman's right-wing, absolutely.

73
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,960
If you read a book like, what is it?

74
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:10,520
It's Naomi Klein, Shock, Soccer, and Not Nanny Wolfe.

75
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,360
So I'm just reading a section where

76
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:14,480
so her take on South Africa.

77
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,400
Her take on South Africa is another case

78
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:20,760
where neoliberal Chicago boys won the country

79
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:22,080
and got everything they wanted.

80
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:26,880
So what are you talking about?

81
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:31,440
But if you're someone who thinks that the slightest concession

82
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,680
to not having a communist revolution

83
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,920
shows that Milton Friedman runs your country,

84
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:37,960
then, of course, you're going to see Milton

85
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:39,440
Friedman ruling the world.

86
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:41,360
Look, Milton Friedman rules every country

87
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:44,840
except North Korea and Cuba and Venezuela.

88
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:49,480
Because they're the only countries that

89
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:52,600
are doing exactly the opposite of what he recommends.

90
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,880
And everybody else is listening to him.

91
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:56,440
Wouldn't it be great if you could just

92
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:58,240
tell your parents this when they say you don't listen to them

93
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:59,080
and you say, no.

94
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:01,040
I mean, I've listened to you like three times,

95
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,640
so you rule my life.

96
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:08,640
So not very compelling, but still.

97
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:11,040
Now, the big clusters of error.

98
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:13,200
So by the way, if you don't want to read the book,

99
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:16,320
you can just go and Google the four cartoons that

100
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:17,840
have been made about the book.

101
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:20,720
So there are four cartoons, one on each of these biases.

102
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,520
So you can just watch the cartoon.

103
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,080
And they're a very well-done cartoon.

104
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:25,320
It's very happy.

105
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:28,720
I'm very flatteringly drawn.

106
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:30,960
Thinner, younger, better dressed than I've

107
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:33,160
ever seen in real life.

108
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:38,120
But so here are the big clusters that I talk about in the book.

109
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,360
The first one I call anti-market bias.

110
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:43,920
So there's a general tendency for non-economists

111
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:46,000
to underestimate the social benefits of the market

112
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:47,400
mechanism.

113
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,920
This does not mean that all economists

114
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:51,080
think markets are always great or anything like that.

115
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:52,000
That's totally wrong.

116
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,760
But still, there's a general tendency

117
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,880
for economists to be more optimistic about markets

118
00:04:55,880 --> 00:04:58,240
to the public, because the public generally

119
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:01,440
looks to see whether someone is trying to make money.

120
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:05,680
And if that is so, lead to a negative inference.

121
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:08,400
On the other hand, economists have almost the opposite

122
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:10,000
heuristic.

123
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,520
For economists, the main concern is to say,

124
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:14,520
well, is there a way for someone to make money

125
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:15,960
solving this problem?

126
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:17,600
The answer is yes, but economists tend not

127
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:18,720
to worry about the problem.

128
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:20,320
Economists worry about problems where

129
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:22,040
they cannot think of a way for someone

130
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:25,040
to profit from a solution.

131
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:26,920
So economists can't say, well, I can't think of any way

132
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:28,680
to make money by cleaning up the air.

133
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:30,440
So that's a problem.

134
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:32,400
But if you can say, well, I can think

135
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,640
of a way for someone to make money by increasing

136
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:36,680
the number of available kidneys, well,

137
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:40,000
then why don't we just have a free market of kidneys?

138
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,600
This is one, unless you'd ever tell you

139
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:43,280
about my dinner with a Marxist economist

140
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:45,920
where we talked about kidney donation.

141
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:47,880
Yes, if you don't remember, so this

142
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,640
was at the New School for Social Research.

143
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:51,480
This is literally an economics department

144
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:55,760
with a six-foot-tall poster of Karl Marx in the public area.

145
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:58,280
It's not just one professor has Marx in his office.

146
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:00,000
It's like, by the water cooler, there's

147
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,640
six-foot-tall Karl Marx.

148
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:05,600
Just the face is six feet tall.

149
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:07,000
And yet, when I had him at dinner,

150
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,400
this is a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity.

151
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:11,000
So what do you guys think about having

152
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,640
a free market of kidneys?

153
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:15,200
And the graduate students were like, well, I can definitely

154
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:16,960
see the argument, but I'm a little worried

155
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,040
that Bohr might not be able to get kidneys.

156
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:21,680
And the Marxist professor said, no, you have to do this.

157
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:24,440
It'll save 10,000 lives a year.

158
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:25,200
Wow.

159
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:28,600
So here's a table of avowed Marxists

160
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,640
who are more in favor of a free market of human kidneys

161
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:36,440
than almost any conservative Republican in the country.

162
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:38,520
And what's the difference?

163
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,760
Economics training is the difference,

164
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:43,920
because they don't just think of prices as redistributive.

165
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:47,000
They think of them as affecting the quantity supplied.

166
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:53,240
So anti-market bias.

167
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:54,360
Then anti-foreign bias.

168
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:55,960
So it's one thing to convince Americans

169
00:06:55,960 --> 00:06:57,160
that when one American buys something

170
00:06:57,160 --> 00:06:59,120
from another American, they're both better off.

171
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:00,480
All right, fine, maybe.

172
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:02,400
But what if an American is buying something

173
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:03,840
from a Chinese?

174
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:08,600
Then, well, we all know what the purpose of this is.

175
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:13,160
It's for the Chinese to trick us, to rip us off,

176
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:14,400
to take advantage of us.

177
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:15,840
That is the whole point.

178
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:18,960
Sure, we get cheap toys, but they get something much better.

179
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:20,440
Green piece of paper.

180
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,680
So that's much more orgies.

181
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:25,520
So that is, again, the underlying idea.

182
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:32,000
So in the book, I go and point out

183
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,920
that there is a thought experiment

184
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:36,160
that economists have been using for quite a while just

185
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:37,800
to illustrate the economist view of trade.

186
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:41,080
And this is very nicely done in the cartoon.

187
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:45,120
But the thought experiment is the Iowa car crop.

188
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:47,200
Actually got a slightly annoyed farmer who says,

189
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:49,120
Iowa does not grow corn, dummy.

190
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:50,880
All right, fine.

191
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:52,680
It's too late to change.

192
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,000
Whatever Iowa grows, I don't know.

193
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,680
Let's just define corn in the 19th century sense.

194
00:07:58,680 --> 00:07:59,880
What you say does grow corn?

195
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:01,800
I'm from Iowa, so I was surrounded by corn.

196
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:02,480
So I don't know.

197
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:04,480
Yeah, so he's right all along.

198
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,880
He pointed me to some statistics on how little corn

199
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:08,440
is grown in Iowa.

200
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:10,880
Anyway, the thought experiment says this.

201
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:13,760
So a scientist shows up and has a press conference

202
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:18,840
and says, I have figured out a way to turn corn into cars.

203
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:20,000
And everyone laughs at him.

204
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,280
That's ridiculous.

205
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:23,680
The chemical components of corn would never

206
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:26,840
allow it to be turned into cars.

207
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:28,960
But then the guy goes and he buys an old factory

208
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:30,160
down by the docks.

209
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:34,080
And lo and behold, corn goes in, cars come out.

210
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:36,640
So everyone says, wow, he has some new chemical process

211
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:40,040
that no one else is smart enough to consider.

212
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:41,240
Or happy.

213
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:42,800
Until an investigative journalist

214
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:46,600
sneaks into the factory and discovers it's only a dock.

215
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:49,000
All that happens is ships with cars for Japan

216
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,760
come in and leave full of corn.

217
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:53,440
That's all that's occurring.

218
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:54,960
And then the guy snaps some pictures,

219
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:56,160
and there's a big scandal.

220
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:58,240
And he says, corn into cars is a fraud.

221
00:08:58,240 --> 00:08:59,840
And then the scientist is arrested

222
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:02,360
for violating every trade law in the books.

223
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:04,920
The end.

224
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:07,600
Now, the point of this thought experiment, of course,

225
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:12,040
is that he did have a technology for turning corn into cars.

226
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:13,720
And what is that technology called?

227
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:15,880
It's called trade.

228
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:17,400
And in fact, trade is a technology

229
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:19,280
for turning anything into anything.

230
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:21,800
It is like the transmuter from Star Trek.

231
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,640
It is that amazing.

232
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:25,880
The only way that it is different

233
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:30,560
is that if it's a transmuter, then what happens

234
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:33,800
is that Americans are better off and no one else is.

235
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:35,480
But on the other hand, if there's trade,

236
00:09:35,480 --> 00:09:38,200
this means that the Japanese are better off as well.

237
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:39,700
So from the American point of view,

238
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:41,840
it doesn't matter how the factory works.

239
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:43,280
But from the human point of view,

240
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:44,800
it does matter because then there's

241
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:47,440
another country where they are better off also,

242
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:51,920
because they are receiving the other side of the deal.

243
00:09:51,920 --> 00:09:54,560
And I've got here a little question.

244
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:56,920
Is anti-corn bearers an aestheticism

245
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:00,000
of anti-free market economics?

246
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:00,880
Well, let's see.

247
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:02,640
I think of it as adding.

248
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:05,680
So you have the general hostility to markets,

249
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,480
and then there's additional hostility

250
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:10,040
when there's foreigners involved,

251
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:12,960
is at least the way that seems to me.

252
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:13,460
Yes?

253
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:15,760
So is the idea of corn for cars that nobody

254
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:18,640
would object to corn for cars if they thought it was actually?

255
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:22,680
Well, not literally nobody, but few.

256
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:24,840
So there are actual Luddites who want

257
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:27,880
to destroy new technologies, but that is a pretty small segment.

258
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:29,920
There's not a lot of political attention

259
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:32,400
being made in saying, let's smash the new machines.

260
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:35,320
Whereas, let's go and cut off trade

261
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:36,960
with a country that's destroying us.

262
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:38,160
Much more popular.

263
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:39,640
Because I feel like you could still

264
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:42,920
set up a battle between the corn lobby

265
00:10:42,920 --> 00:10:46,520
and then better auto workers, because there would still be.

266
00:10:46,520 --> 00:10:47,020
Right.

267
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:50,320
Like, what do people without a dog in the fight think?

268
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:53,240
What does just a random American looking on the sidelines think?

269
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:55,600
If someone says, there's a new machine that works great,

270
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:58,120
it should be destroyed to save jobs,

271
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:00,320
there's a little bit of appeal of that.

272
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:02,800
But there's something like, I don't know,

273
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,800
wouldn't we have to go and do other things too

274
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:06,760
to be consistent if we believe that?

275
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:08,960
Wouldn't we have to destroy our entire civilization?

276
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:13,360
I don't think there's too much to spoil

277
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:16,200
in saying the final episode of Battle Sword Galactica,

278
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:19,840
they make a decision to destroy all their technology

279
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:21,840
and then become cavemen.

280
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:23,620
And then they become our ancestors.

281
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:25,360
And just to make sure there's no evidence,

282
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:26,920
they throw it all into the sun.

283
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:28,960
So there's no sign of it ever happening.

284
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:31,640
It sounds like a spoiler to me.

285
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:36,600
Well, the show has been out and has been done for 10 years.

286
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:38,840
I mean, the only spoiler is, do you really

287
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:41,640
want to watch a show that has what the conclusion is back,

288
00:11:41,640 --> 00:11:42,140
idiot?

289
00:11:42,140 --> 00:11:43,120
I'm just kidding.

290
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:44,080
I'm just kidding.

291
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:44,880
I'm just kidding.

292
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:46,320
I'm just kidding.

293
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:49,360
So let's become cavemen.

294
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,920
Let's try that.

295
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:53,880
And let's make sure it's irreversible too,

296
00:11:53,880 --> 00:11:56,560
just to make sure that no one ever tries going back

297
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:00,680
to not being a caveman.

298
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:04,120
And as you may have heard that I have a big interest

299
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:06,080
in open immigration.

300
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:08,880
And when people say, well, why is it

301
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:10,640
that everyone's so wrong and you're right?

302
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:13,320
I'm like, yeah, anti-foreign bias.

303
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:14,880
Anti-foreign bias.

304
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:17,280
When people economically interact with foreigners,

305
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:20,760
they are prone to paranoid pessimism

306
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:23,200
and to seeing the slightest negative thing

307
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,640
as being some kind of proof that it shouldn't happen.

308
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:31,440
I mean, just think about the sheer innumeracy

309
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:32,720
of popular action.

310
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:34,560
So suppose someone were to say, look,

311
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:37,680
if we go and open up our borders,

312
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:42,120
there's going to be a terror attack every year.

313
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:44,520
Now, how much economic gain will you

314
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:46,600
have to posit before people say, eh, it's worth it?

315
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,000
A trillion dollars a year.

316
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,920
Is that enough to make it worthwhile to have

317
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:56,200
one terrorist attack a year?

318
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:57,840
How about $10 trillion?

319
00:12:57,840 --> 00:13:00,240
How about $100 trillion?

320
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:03,080
This is one where I think it's likely for a lot of people,

321
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:06,560
there is no known finite number that would get

322
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:08,480
people to say it's worth it.

323
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:11,360
Even though, of course, people bet their lives all the time

324
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:13,600
for far lesser gain.

325
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:14,800
What's the difference?

326
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:18,320
Foreigners are involved.

327
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:20,440
So similarly, suppose someone were to say, look,

328
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:22,240
we can get rid of all terrorism if we just

329
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:25,280
go and execute the following 10,000 Americans.

330
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:26,720
We come up with a computer, these

331
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:28,600
are the 10,000 most likely to do it.

332
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:30,880
And actually, we got a pure prediction.

333
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:33,880
And suppose the critics look at it and say, yeah, it's right.

334
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:36,760
So execute 10,000 Americans and end the solve the terror

335
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:37,920
problem.

336
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:40,120
Sound like a good deal?

337
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:41,520
Wait, are these American citizens

338
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:42,720
we're talking about killing?

339
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:45,720
No, no, then we can't do it.

340
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:47,960
Suppose there were even 10 American citizens,

341
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:50,160
but they're inside the borders of the US.

342
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:51,920
And there's no proof of anything at all.

343
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:52,640
They haven't done anything.

344
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:53,800
It's just pre-crime.

345
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:57,800
It's just the computer predicts that they're high risk.

346
00:13:57,800 --> 00:13:59,720
So quite possibly, nothing would be done.

347
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:03,920
But suppose you have to leave a million refugees in a war zone.

348
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:06,920
They will be exterminated.

349
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:10,080
Well, but maybe there would have been terrorism.

350
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:11,640
Prove there wouldn't be.

351
00:14:11,640 --> 00:14:14,440
That's kind of hard.

352
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:17,480
So that's my anti-foreign bias story.

353
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:20,120
Then I also have make-word bias.

354
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,800
A tendency to rate economic performance based upon jobs

355
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:26,200
rather than production, which again,

356
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:29,800
is a very emotionally easy, especially during recession.

357
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:32,200
And yet, you all probably heard, imagine

358
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:35,720
that we were to go and judge every new technology based

359
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:38,000
upon its effect upon employment.

360
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,440
How many great technologies would not be allowed?

361
00:14:42,440 --> 00:14:44,680
Basically, actually, relative to what

362
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:47,680
the economy would look like a century ago,

363
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:49,800
then there's probably almost no jobs that have not

364
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:51,400
been destroyed by technology.

365
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:56,520
Because almost all of us would be working in agriculture.

366
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:59,640
Because as the expansion, as technology

367
00:14:59,640 --> 00:15:02,040
improved agriculture, it's created a lot of other jobs.

368
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:04,640
And then, of course, many of those jobs have been destroyed.

369
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:05,520
And so on.

370
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:09,840
So this usually does not actually

371
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:15,480
lead to laws banning technological improvements.

372
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:18,200
Although it probably does lead to hyperregulation

373
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:20,040
of new technologies.

374
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:21,960
So a standard point in risk regulation

375
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:25,480
is that driverless cars are held to a way higher standard

376
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:28,880
than drivered cars.

377
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:30,680
So a driverless car kills one person,

378
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:34,040
then people are like, oh, we may need to rethink this.

379
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:37,600
How many people were killed in regular cars that day?

380
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:39,200
Statistically, about 100 people were

381
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:41,840
killed in regular cars in America on that day.

382
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,520
I think actually it's not as bad anymore.

383
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:44,120
It's like 50.

384
00:15:44,120 --> 00:15:45,480
Now I'm not like 50.

385
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:47,920
But still, 50 times as many people were killed.

386
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:48,840
On that single day.

387
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:51,320
So how come those stories aren't headlines?

388
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:54,760
Well, the risk that we have, that's just part of the world.

389
00:15:54,760 --> 00:15:57,960
Whereas the risks that of new technology,

390
00:15:57,960 --> 00:15:59,960
that could be stopped.

391
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:02,640
And so it's very important not to allow it

392
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:04,440
until we're absolutely sure.

393
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:07,120
What if one of those cars kills you?

394
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:10,440
And again, proper opportunity cost reasoning response

395
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:11,880
to that.

396
00:16:11,880 --> 00:16:13,520
We shouldn't allow driverless cars

397
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:15,040
because one of them might kill you.

398
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:18,760
What if one of those cars would kill someone you love?

399
00:16:18,760 --> 00:16:20,480
What is the numerate response to that?

400
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:27,560
What if a regular car kills someone you love?

401
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:30,000
It's like, well, you can't worry about that kind of thing.

402
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,080
You'll never leave the house if you think that way.

403
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:36,520
So silly stuff.

404
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:39,560
So I think you probably see neighborhood bias most strongly

405
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:42,600
in laws that try to prevent firing,

406
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:45,720
try to prevent layoffs, try to prevent plant closures.

407
00:16:45,720 --> 00:16:47,680
So things like that where there's

408
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:50,360
a set of laws designed to go and freeze existing employment

409
00:16:50,360 --> 00:16:52,560
in place on the theory that if those jobs are lost,

410
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:55,000
that they'll never be replaced.

411
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
And again, also the idea of the point of a job

412
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,200
is to give someone an income.

413
00:16:58,200 --> 00:16:59,600
It's not to produce stuff.

414
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:00,680
But again, of course, everyone had

415
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:03,140
a job that gave them an income and didn't produce anything.

416
00:17:03,140 --> 00:17:06,160
What would those incomes buy?

417
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:08,080
There'd be nothing to buy.

418
00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:10,040
And the whole idea of giving people an income

419
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:13,560
is parasitic upon production, which

420
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:16,640
brings me to my fundamental truth of economics,

421
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:19,100
that if people learn nothing else, they should learn that.

422
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:19,960
Since you're getting a PhD, you should

423
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:21,440
learn more than one truth.

424
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:25,920
But the truth is, if there's one lesson that I wanted people

425
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:30,200
to know above all others, the secret of mass consumption

426
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:32,320
is mass production.

427
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:35,800
Always focus on what causes production.

428
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:37,880
And all the complexities, just forget about them

429
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:39,500
because they are going to mislead you.

430
00:17:39,500 --> 00:17:42,640
Just ask, will this increase the production of the world?

431
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:44,240
Then thumbs up.

432
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:47,120
This is pushing the world in the direction of prosperity.

433
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:51,720
And then a final big error that I

434
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:53,840
talked about, pessimistic bias, is a general tendency

435
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:55,760
to see a world getting worse, bad now,

436
00:17:55,760 --> 00:17:57,880
and going to be even worse in the future.

437
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:00,080
This is, again, a big gap between economists

438
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:03,520
and the public, where economists at least

439
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:06,040
tend to see things as being not as bad as the public.

440
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:07,960
So even during a recession, a tendency

441
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:10,600
for the public to just think this is a permanent disaster,

442
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:12,920
or they will never improve.

443
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:16,000
And again, classic economist answer is,

444
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:17,480
this recession will end.

445
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:20,960
Every single previous one has people.

446
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:23,600
And what is a normal person's retort

447
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:26,840
to an economist pointing out a universal regularity

448
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:29,480
in the American economy?

449
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:30,840
This time it's different.

450
00:18:30,840 --> 00:18:31,840
Oh, you don't understand.

451
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:33,280
This time there's new technology.

452
00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:36,120
It's destroying jobs permanently.

453
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:37,120
All right.

454
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,400
And to me, the worst thing is when

455
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:45,080
economists turn against science itself

456
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:49,320
and make a generalization based upon the opposite of what

457
00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:50,920
everything has occurred.

458
00:18:50,920 --> 00:18:53,560
Yes, so I did tell you about my bet with Tyler Cowen

459
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:54,400
about unemployment.

460
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:56,280
Always worth repeating the story.

461
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:59,080
Yes, so during the last recession,

462
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:01,800
he had a book called The Great Stagnation,

463
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:03,920
and then followed up with Average is Over.

464
00:19:03,920 --> 00:19:07,440
Both of them on the theme of, due to fundamental changes

465
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:09,200
in the real economy, the unemployment rate

466
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:11,560
is going to stay high for the indefinite future.

467
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:13,880
It's not just a short run recession,

468
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:16,520
despite the fact that it's always been that way before.

469
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:20,120
And he gave me 10 to 1 odds that the US unemployment rate would

470
00:19:20,120 --> 00:19:23,840
not fall below 5% for the next 20 years,

471
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:27,040
and he lost in less than three years.

472
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:28,320
And what did I do?

473
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:31,080
I just bet upon history.

474
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:34,160
And in fact, I bet upon a very general pattern.

475
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:37,240
If you just go and look at all US unemployment data ever,

476
00:19:37,240 --> 00:19:41,800
a very general pattern is that once the US unemployment rate

477
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:45,760
peaks, it goes back to, it falls by about 1 percentage point

478
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:49,360
per year, with a standard deviation of 0.2 percentage

479
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:50,960
points.

480
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:53,440
So really, if you just go and take a ruler

481
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:56,600
and look at the slopes of the declines,

482
00:19:56,600 --> 00:20:00,160
you can move the ruler almost in parallel across the data


